# Zero to five, counted on the ten frame.
lesson language-one-to-five v=1 format=teach grade=1 unit=4 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "[[num.one|cap]], [[num.two]], [[num.three]]"
  goal "Count from zero to five in [[language.name]] and name how many there are."

  look {
    scene 37x8 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        row counts space=0 align=end {
          tenframe one count=1 color=sky
          tenframe three count=3 color=mint
          tenframe five count=5 color=tang
        }
      }
    }
    say """
      Count: [[num.one]], [[num.two]], [[num.three]], [[num.four]], [[num.five]]. None at all is
      [[num.zero]]. The first frame has [[num.one]], the next has [[num.three]] and the last has
      [[num.five]]. Say them with a finger for each counter.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.one-to-five-name k=3
    }
    show language.one-to-five-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show language.one-to-five-meaning k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.one-to-five-write k=1 easy-k=2 hard-k=3
    show language.one-to-five-name k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.one-to-five-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.one-to-five-write k=4 hard-k=2
    show language.one-to-five-meaning k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.one-to-five-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.one-to-five-write k=1
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show language.one-to-five-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.one-to-five-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[num.one|cap]], [[num.two]], [[num.three]], [[num.four]], [[num.five]]. None at all is [[num.zero]]."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is saying the number words as a chant without matching one word to
    one thing, and mixing [[num.four]] with [[num.five]]. Ask the child to touch each counter as
    they say its word. The frame holds the count, so every question can be checked by counting in
    English first and then finding the word. [[num.five|cap]] is said [[num.five|say]]. If the child
    is stuck, count in English, then say the same number in the language together. Off the page,
    count the stairs, and count them back down. It meets England's key stage 2 languages programme
    (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "explore the patterns and sounds of language through songs
    and rhymes and link the spelling, sound and meaning of words"; Japan's grades 3 and 4 foreign
    language activities: "Activities to talk about the numbers and figures, etc. of surroundings
    while showing the actual objects"; and CEFR A1, "Can understand and use familiar everyday
    expressions and very basic phrases". The phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[num.zero]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[num.one]] and [[num.two]] and the words to write are [[num.three]] and [[num.four]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[num.four]] and [[num.five]] and the words to write are [[num.zero]], [[num.three]] and [[num.two]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
